  "interpretations": {
    "class_counts": "Only ~7% of images are healthy leaves. Accuracy alone would hide a model that ignores this class, so the model is trained with class weights and judged on macro-F1.",
    "green_ratio": "Healthy leaves are measurably greener (median G/(R+G+B) ~0.435 vs ~0.40). Disease destroys chlorophyll. This separates healthy from diseased, but not early from late blight.",
    "lesion_fraction": "Healthy leaves have almost no yellow/brown tissue (~1%), while blighted leaves have 16-20% (median). Early blight varies most, from a few spots to large yellowed areas.",
    "texture": "Early blight's concentric 'target' spots create the most edges; late blight's large, soft, water-soaked patches are the smoothest. Texture is what tells the two blights apart.",
    "brightness": "Early-blight leaves are brightest (yellowing); late-blight leaves darkest (necrotic lesions)."
  }
